FINDINGS:

1. [MAJOR] src/runtime.rs:349-355 — `in` was changed from `items.contains(&left)` to calling `equality`, which returns `Err` for any mixed-kind pair (`mixed_kinds` at src/runtime.rs:436-444). This is broader than decimals: `time.date(2024, 1, 1) in [5, time.date(2024, 1, 1)]` now aborts with `Cannot compare a date with a number` instead of answering `yes`, and `date in [5]` changes from `no` to a runtime error. SPEC.md:407 says `in` "answers `yes` when the left side equals one of its elements by value", and the phase only claims decimal/number mixing. Fix: keep membership boolean (use a membership-specific comparison that treats other-kind elements as unequal) or add date/duration membership tests and a SPEC decision. Checked by tracing `binary_op` → `equality` → `mixed_kinds`; no existing test pins mixed-kind membership.

2. [MINOR] src/decimal.rs:109-119 — `Decimal::parse` accumulates the magnitude in a positive `i128` and only then negates it, so `decimal.from_text("-170141183460469231731687303715884105728")` is rejected as "too large" even though that value is exactly `i128::MIN` and is representable (`mantissa_from_magnitude` at src/decimal.rs:610-624 explicitly accepts that magnitude for the negative sign). The same asymmetry appears in `Wai::to_i128` at src/decimal.rs:468-474, so a negative quotient of magnitude `2^127` is refused. Fix: accumulate/convert the magnitude as `u128` or special-case `i128::MIN`, and add a boundary test for negative minimum text and quotient.

3. [MINOR] src/runtime.rs:449-451 — `mixed_kinds` recurses into lists with `a.iter().zip(b.iter())`, so only the first `min(len)` positions are checked. `[1, decimal.from_text("1")] is [1]` returns `no` instead of the mixed-kind error the function's own contract promises ("a list holding a decimal and one holding a number are just as mixed as their elements"). Fix: scan each side's elements for direct mixed kinds, or iterate to the longer length, and add an unequal-length list test.

REVIEW VERDICT: FINDINGS 3
